**One finding, three ids.** Suppose a banking app unlocks its vault when a fingerprint callback says "success", without any key being involved (lesson 10). A report names it three ways:

- the **control** it breaks: MASVS-AUTH-2, "The app performs local authentication securely according to the platform best practices";
- the **test** that found it: MASTG-TEST-0327, *References to APIs for Event-Bound Biometric Authentication* (Android);
- the **weakness**: MASWE-0020, *Local Authentication Can Be Bypassed*, so developers can look up the general pattern.

You will also meet the **OWASP Mobile Top 10** (2024 edition: M1 Improper Credential Usage, M2 Inadequate Supply Chain Security, M3 Insecure Authentication/Authorization, M4 Insufficient Input/Output Validation, M5 Insecure Communication, M6 Inadequate Privacy Controls, M7 Insufficient Binary Protections, M8 Security Misconfiguration, M9 Insecure Data Storage, M10 Insufficient Cryptography). It is an awareness list of risks. MASVS is the testable standard; use the Top 10 to explain, MASVS and MASTG to test.

